&lt;p&gt;# Comments: 0&lt;/p&gt;</description><guid isPermaLink="false">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50651501</guid><pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 04:42:59 +0000</pubDate></item><item><title>Show HN: Talira – Search engine for software, built for AI agents</title><link>https://talira.ai/</link><description>&lt;p&gt;Hi HN, I'm Nathan. I wanted to share a project that I've been working on this month.&lt;p&gt;A few weeks ago, my Rust target folders had grown to about 70 GB across many git worktrees, and I asked Claude to figure out how to stop that from happening. It couldn't find much online, so we started to build our own workaround using some unstable cargo flags. Then someone on r/rust asked why we hadn't just used kache. Someone else mentioned Mr Boxington on a GitHub issue (another great solution).&lt;p&gt;I also got annoyed that my Claude Code sessions would constantly ask me to reauthenticate my MCP servers, which happens when you run a lot of them in parallel. An agent couldn't find any good "MCP proxy" so we started planning and building our own. I searched Google myself and found MCPProxy.app. It worked pretty well so I wrote a blog post and shared it on X. Some people replied and told me that Executor.sh was an even better solution. They were right, so I switched to Executor.&lt;p&gt;After all this, I decided to build Talira: A search engine for software that an AI agent can check before it starts building your own solution. You describe the problem in plain words ("my Rust target folders use 80 GB across git worktrees"). Talira searches the catalog using vector embeddings and uses Jev for filtering and reranking.&lt;p&gt;To try it:&lt;p&gt;- Search on &lt;a href="https://talira.ai" rel="nofollow"&gt;https://talira.ai&lt;/a&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Add the MCP server to Claude Code: claude mcp add --transport http --scope user talira &lt;a href="https://mcp.talira.ai/mcp" rel="nofollow"&gt;https://mcp.talira.ai/mcp&lt;/a&gt;&lt;p&gt;Your agent can also send a report after each search: the problem in general terms, what you tried, what you kept and why. The idea is that it can eventually become a crowd-sourced database of tools that people actually chose and continued using over time.&lt;p&gt;It's currently in beta and the catalog still has a lot of gaps. I should also mention that I'm aware that ChatGPT and Claude already know a lot and are already very good at search. So there are plenty of cases where you don't need Talira at all. I'll need to collect some more real-world searches before I can benchmark it properly to see if it makes any difference.&lt;p&gt;I'd like to know if you've ever had any similar experiences. Let me know if an AI wasn't able to find a good solution that already existed.&lt;p&gt;Thanks for reading!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
